---
title: Hotkeys Guide
id: hotkeys
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" hook is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in React applications. It wraps the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyManager"},{"type":"text","value":" with automatic lifecycle management, stale-closure prevention, and React ref support."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical keys and physical positions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Binding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity checked"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ key: 'S', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.key"},{"type":"text","value":", with conservative code fallback"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.code"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Enter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical Enter, including numpad Enter"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[NumpadEnter]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate physical Enter positions"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[F13]"},{"type":"text","value":". Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" field; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":". A binding has either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":", never both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a layout where the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyQ"},{"type":"text","value":" position produces "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"A"},{"type":"text","value":" follows that character and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[KeyQ]"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks expose the same distinction in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.parsedHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":": check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parsed.code !== undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" before reading its physical identity. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatForDisplay"},{"type":"text","value":" for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useHotkey } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  useHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The callback receives the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyboardEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" as the first argument and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyCallbackContext"},{"type":"text","value":" as the second:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pass a hotkey as a string or as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RawHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" object (modifier booleans optional). Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mod"},{"type":"text","value":" for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\nuseHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\nuseHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"changing-a-binding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changing a binding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"updating-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updating options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass current options when the component renders. Hooks synchronize those options with the existing registration. You do not need to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" in application code. Provider defaults and recorder options follow the same component update lifecycle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property getters are read when the hook runs. They do not subscribe to external state independently of the component. Keep changing values in framework state so the component updates, and avoid creating an options object once with an initial state snapshot."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you register a hotkey without options, or omit specific ones, these defaults apply:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"why-these-defaults","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why these defaults?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most hotkey registrations exist to override the browser. When you bind "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":" to save a document, you don't want the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog too. So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preventDefault"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopPropagation"},{"type":"text","value":" are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"smart-input-handling-with-ignoreinputs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Smart input handling with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ctrl"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Meta"},{"type":"text","value":" shortcuts (like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":") and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Escape"},{"type":"text","value":" fire even while focus is inside a text field or textarea, so save and close work wherever the user happens to be. Single keys and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt"},{"type":"text","value":" combos are ignored inside non-button inputs, because those are just typing. Button-type inputs ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type=\"button\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"submit\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"reset\""},{"type":"text","value":") don't block any hotkeys."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"hotkey-conflicts-and-conflictbehavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkey conflicts and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you register a hotkey that's already registered somewhere else in your app, the library logs a warning by default ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior: 'warn'"},{"type":"text","value":"). That surfaces accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global defaults via provider"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can change the default options for all "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":". Per-hook options will override the provider defaults."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hotkey-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkey options"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enabled","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabled hotkeys stay registered in the manager and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed. The hook updates "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" on the existing registration instead of unregistering and re-registering."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [isEditing, setIsEditing] = useState(false)\n\n// Only active when editing\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { enabled: isEditing })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"preventdefault","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preventDefault"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatically calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.preventDefault()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the hotkey fires. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Browser default is prevented by default\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\n\n// Opt out when you want the browser's default behavior\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"stoppropagation","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopPropagation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.stopPropagation()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the hotkey fires. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Event propagation is stopped by default\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\n\n// Opt out when you need the event to bubble\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"eventtype","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eventType"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether to listen on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keydown"},{"type":"text","value":" (default) or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyup"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Fire when the key is released\nuseHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"requirereset","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requireReset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", the hotkey will only fire once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Only fires once per Escape press, not on key repeat\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"ignoreinputs","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", the hotkey will not fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. Button-type inputs ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type=\"button\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"submit\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"reset\""},{"type":"text","value":") are not ignored, so shortcuts like Mod+S work when the user has tabbed to a form button. When unset, a smart default applies: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ctrl"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Meta"},{"type":"text","value":" shortcuts and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Escape"},{"type":"text","value":" fire in inputs; single keys and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt"},{"type":"text","value":" combos are ignored."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Single key - ignored in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\n\n// Mod+S and Escape - fire in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeDialog())\n\n// Override: force a single key to fire in inputs\nuseHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs: false"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly to override the smart default."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"target","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document"},{"type":"text","value":". Can be a DOM element, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":", or a React ref."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useRef } from 'react'\n\nfunction Panel() {\n  const panelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)\n\n  // Only listens for events on this specific element\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { target: panelRef })\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={panelRef} tabIndex={0}>\n      <p>Panel content</p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using a ref as the target, make sure the element is focusable (has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tabIndex"},{"type":"text","value":") so it can receive keyboard events."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflictbehavior","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'warn'"},{"type":"text","value":" (default) - Logs a warning but allows the registration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'error'"},{"type":"text","value":" - Throws an error"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'replace'"},{"type":"text","value":" - Replaces the existing registration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'allow'"},{"type":"text","value":" - Allows multiple registrations silently"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"platform","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"platform"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Override the auto-detected platform. Useful for testing or for applications that need to force a specific platform behavior."}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"stale-closure-prevention","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stale closure prevention"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" hook automatically syncs the callback on every render, so you never need to worry about stale closures:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n\n  // This callback always has access to the latest `count` value\n  useHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count)\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-cleanup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic cleanup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hook automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function TemporaryPanel() {\n  // Automatically cleaned up when this component unmounts\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering multiple hotkeys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic list whose length isn't known at compile time, use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" (plural) hook instead of calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" multiple times. You can't call hooks conditionally or in loops, so a single hook that takes an array is the way to handle a variable number of shortcuts."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useHotkeys } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  useHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic hotkey lists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a plain array, you can derive it from data:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function MenuShortcuts({ items }) {\n  useHotkeys(\n    items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hook diffs the array between renders by array index plus the normalized hotkey string, registering new hotkeys and unregistering removed ones automatically. Reordering the array changes that identity, so reordered entries are unregistered and re-registered even if their callback references stay the same."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata (name, description, and group)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every hotkey registration can carry a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" object with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":". Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option is typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", which ships with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"declare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"introspecting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introspecting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeyRegistrations"},{"type":"text","value":" hook to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. It's the building block for shortcut palettes and help dialogs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const { hotkeys, sequences } = useHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        {hotkeys.map((reg) => (\n          <li key={reg.id}>\n            <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n            {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n            {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      {sequences.length > 0 && (\n        <>\n          <h2>Sequences</h2>\n          <ul>\n            {sequences.map((reg) => (\n              <li key={reg.id}>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            ))}\n          </ul>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" array contains registration objects with the hotkey string, options (including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":"), and enabled state. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequences"},{"type":"text","value":" array contains sequence registrations with the same structure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-hotkey-manager","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hotkey manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under the hood, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyManager"},{"type":"text","value":". You can access the manager directly if needed:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\n\n// Check if a hotkey is registered\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\n\n// Get total number of registrations\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The manager attaches event listeners per target element, so only elements with registered hotkeys get listeners. That beats a single global listener that has to inspect every keystroke."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","text":"Logical keys and physical positions","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"changing-a-binding","text":"Changing a binding","level":3},{"id":"updating-options","text":"Updating options","level":2},{"id":"default-options","text":"Default options","level":2},{"id":"why-these-defaults","text":"Why these defaults?","level":3},{"id":"smart-input-handling-with-ignoreinputs","text":"Smart input handling with ignoreInputs","level":4},{"id":"hotkey-conflicts-and-conflictbehavior","text":"Hotkey conflicts and conflictBehavior","level":4},{"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","text":"Global defaults via provider","level":3},{"id":"hotkey-options","text":"Hotkey options","level":2},{"id":"enabled","text":"enabled","level":3},{"id":"preventdefault","text":"preventDefault","level":3},{"id":"stoppropagation","text":"stopPropagation","level":3},{"id":"eventtype","text":"eventType","level":3},{"id":"requirereset","text":"requireReset","level":3},{"id":"ignoreinputs","text":"ignoreInputs","level":3},{"id":"target","text":"target","level":3},{"id":"conflictbehavior","text":"conflictBehavior","level":3},{"id":"platform","text":"platform","level":3},{"id":"stale-closure-prevention","text":"Stale closure prevention","level":2},{"id":"automatic-cleanup","text":"Automatic cleanup","level":2},{"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","text":"Registering multiple hotkeys","level":2},{"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","text":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides","level":3},{"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","text":"Dynamic hotkey lists","level":3},{"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","text":"Metadata (name, description, and group)","level":2},{"id":"introspecting-registrations","text":"Introspecting registrations","level":2},{"id":"the-hotkey-manager","text":"The hotkey manager","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe `useHotkey` hook is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in React applications. It wraps the singleton `HotkeyManager` with automatic lifecycle management, stale-closure prevention, and React ref support.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkey } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  useHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})\n```\n\nYou can pass a hotkey as a string or as a `RawHotkey` object (modifier booleans optional). Use `mod` for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\nuseHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\nuseHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Updating options\n\nPass current options when the component renders. Hooks synchronize those options with the existing registration. You do not need to call `setOptions` in application code. Provider defaults and recorder options follow the same component update lifecycle.\n\nProperty getters are read when the hook runs. They do not subscribe to external state independently of the component. Keep changing values in framework state so the component updates, and avoid creating an options object once with an initial state snapshot.\n\n## Default options\n\nWhen you register a hotkey without options, or omit specific ones, these defaults apply:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})\n```\n\n### Why these defaults?\n\nMost hotkey registrations exist to override the browser. When you bind `Mod+S` to save a document, you don't want the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog too. So `preventDefault` and `stopPropagation` are `true` by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior.\n\n#### Smart input handling with `ignoreInputs`\n\nBy default, `Ctrl`/`Meta` shortcuts (like `Mod+S`) and `Escape` fire even while focus is inside a text field or textarea, so save and close work wherever the user happens to be. Single keys and `Shift`/`Alt` combos are ignored inside non-button inputs, because those are just typing. Button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, `\"reset\"`) don't block any hotkeys.\n\n#### Hotkey conflicts and `conflictBehavior`\n\nIf you register a hotkey that's already registered somewhere else in your app, the library logs a warning by default (`conflictBehavior: 'warn'`). That surfaces accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can change the default options for all `useHotkey` calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-hook options will override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n## Hotkey options\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to `true`.\n\nDisabled hotkeys stay registered in the manager and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed. The hook updates `enabled` on the existing registration instead of unregistering and re-registering.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isEditing, setIsEditing] = useState(false)\n\n// Only active when editing\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { enabled: isEditing })\n```\n\n### `preventDefault`\n\nAutomatically calls `event.preventDefault()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\n// Browser default is prevented by default\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\n\n// Opt out when you want the browser's default behavior\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })\n```\n\n### `stopPropagation`\n\nCalls `event.stopPropagation()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\n// Event propagation is stopped by default\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\n\n// Opt out when you need the event to bubble\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })\n```\n\n### `eventType`\n\nWhether to listen on `keydown` (default) or `keyup`.\n\n```tsx\n// Fire when the key is released\nuseHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })\n```\n\n### `requireReset`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey will only fire once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to `false`.\n\n```tsx\n// Only fires once per Escape press, not on key repeat\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey will not fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. Button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, `\"reset\"`) are not ignored, so shortcuts like Mod+S work when the user has tabbed to a form button. When unset, a smart default applies: `Ctrl`/`Meta` shortcuts and `Escape` fire in inputs; single keys and `Shift`/`Alt` combos are ignored.\n\n```tsx\n// Single key - ignored in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\n\n// Mod+S and Escape - fire in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeDialog())\n\n// Override: force a single key to fire in inputs\nuseHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\nSet `ignoreInputs: false` or `true` explicitly to override the smart default.\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be a DOM element, `document`, `window`, or a React ref.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useRef } from 'react'\n\nfunction Panel() {\n  const panelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)\n\n  // Only listens for events on this specific element\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { target: panelRef })\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={panelRef} tabIndex={0}>\n      <p>Panel content</p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using a ref as the target, make sure the element is focusable (has `tabIndex`) so it can receive keyboard events.\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\nControls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options:\n\n- `'warn'` (default) - Logs a warning but allows the registration\n- `'error'` - Throws an error\n- `'replace'` - Replaces the existing registration\n- `'allow'` - Allows multiple registrations silently\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\nOverride the auto-detected platform. Useful for testing or for applications that need to force a specific platform behavior.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Stale closure prevention\n\nThe `useHotkey` hook automatically syncs the callback on every render, so you never need to worry about stale closures:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n\n  // This callback always has access to the latest `count` value\n  useHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count)\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nThe hook automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts:\n\n```tsx\nfunction TemporaryPanel() {\n  // Automatically cleaned up when this component unmounts\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}\n```\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nTo register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic list whose length isn't known at compile time, use the `useHotkeys` (plural) hook instead of calling `useHotkey` multiple times. You can't call hooks conditionally or in loops, so a single hook that takes an array is the way to handle a variable number of shortcuts.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeys } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  useHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nBecause `useHotkeys` accepts a plain array, you can derive it from data:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MenuShortcuts({ items }) {\n  useHotkeys(\n    items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe hook diffs the array between renders by array index plus the normalized hotkey string, registering new hotkeys and unregistering removed ones automatically. Reordering the array changes that identity, so reordered entries are unregistered and re-registered even if their callback references stay the same.\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```tsx\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `useHotkeyRegistrations` hook to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. It's the building block for shortcut palettes and help dialogs.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const { hotkeys, sequences } = useHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        {hotkeys.map((reg) => (\n          <li key={reg.id}>\n            <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n            {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n            {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      {sequences.length > 0 && (\n        <>\n          <h2>Sequences</h2>\n          <ul>\n            {sequences.map((reg) => (\n              <li key={reg.id}>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            ))}\n          </ul>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe returned `hotkeys` array contains registration objects with the hotkey string, options (including `meta`), and enabled state. The `sequences` array contains sequence registrations with the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nUnder the hood, `useHotkey` uses the singleton `HotkeyManager`. You can access the manager directly if needed:\n\n```tsx\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\n\n// Check if a hotkey is registered\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\n\n// Get total number of registrations\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n\nThe manager attaches event listeners per target element, so only elements with registered hotkeys get listeners. That beats a single global listener that has to inspect every keystroke.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Hotkeys Guide\nid: hotkeys\n---\n\nThe `useHotkey` hook is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in React applications. It wraps the singleton `HotkeyManager` with automatic lifecycle management, stale-closure prevention, and React ref support.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkey } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  useHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})\n```\n\nYou can pass a hotkey as a string or as a `RawHotkey` object (modifier booleans optional). Use `mod` for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\nuseHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\nuseHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\nuseHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Updating options\n\nPass current options when the component renders. Hooks synchronize those options with the existing registration. You do not need to call `setOptions` in application code. Provider defaults and recorder options follow the same component update lifecycle.\n\nProperty getters are read when the hook runs. They do not subscribe to external state independently of the component. Keep changing values in framework state so the component updates, and avoid creating an options object once with an initial state snapshot.\n\n## Default options\n\nWhen you register a hotkey without options, or omit specific ones, these defaults apply:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})\n```\n\n### Why these defaults?\n\nMost hotkey registrations exist to override the browser. When you bind `Mod+S` to save a document, you don't want the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog too. So `preventDefault` and `stopPropagation` are `true` by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior.\n\n#### Smart input handling with `ignoreInputs`\n\nBy default, `Ctrl`/`Meta` shortcuts (like `Mod+S`) and `Escape` fire even while focus is inside a text field or textarea, so save and close work wherever the user happens to be. Single keys and `Shift`/`Alt` combos are ignored inside non-button inputs, because those are just typing. Button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, `\"reset\"`) don't block any hotkeys.\n\n#### Hotkey conflicts and `conflictBehavior`\n\nIf you register a hotkey that's already registered somewhere else in your app, the library logs a warning by default (`conflictBehavior: 'warn'`). That surfaces accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can change the default options for all `useHotkey` calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-hook options will override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n## Hotkey options\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to `true`.\n\nDisabled hotkeys stay registered in the manager and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed. The hook updates `enabled` on the existing registration instead of unregistering and re-registering.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isEditing, setIsEditing] = useState(false)\n\n// Only active when editing\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { enabled: isEditing })\n```\n\n### `preventDefault`\n\nAutomatically calls `event.preventDefault()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\n// Browser default is prevented by default\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\n\n// Opt out when you want the browser's default behavior\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })\n```\n\n### `stopPropagation`\n\nCalls `event.stopPropagation()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\n// Event propagation is stopped by default\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\n\n// Opt out when you need the event to bubble\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })\n```\n\n### `eventType`\n\nWhether to listen on `keydown` (default) or `keyup`.\n\n```tsx\n// Fire when the key is released\nuseHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })\n```\n\n### `requireReset`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey will only fire once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to `false`.\n\n```tsx\n// Only fires once per Escape press, not on key repeat\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey will not fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. Button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, `\"reset\"`) are not ignored, so shortcuts like Mod+S work when the user has tabbed to a form button. When unset, a smart default applies: `Ctrl`/`Meta` shortcuts and `Escape` fire in inputs; single keys and `Shift`/`Alt` combos are ignored.\n\n```tsx\n// Single key - ignored in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\n\n// Mod+S and Escape - fire in inputs by default (smart default)\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\nuseHotkey('Escape', () => closeDialog())\n\n// Override: force a single key to fire in inputs\nuseHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\nSet `ignoreInputs: false` or `true` explicitly to override the smart default.\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be a DOM element, `document`, `window`, or a React ref.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useRef } from 'react'\n\nfunction Panel() {\n  const panelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)\n\n  // Only listens for events on this specific element\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { target: panelRef })\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={panelRef} tabIndex={0}>\n      <p>Panel content</p>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using a ref as the target, make sure the element is focusable (has `tabIndex`) so it can receive keyboard events.\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\nControls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options:\n\n- `'warn'` (default) - Logs a warning but allows the registration\n- `'error'` - Throws an error\n- `'replace'` - Replaces the existing registration\n- `'allow'` - Allows multiple registrations silently\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\nOverride the auto-detected platform. Useful for testing or for applications that need to force a specific platform behavior.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Stale closure prevention\n\nThe `useHotkey` hook automatically syncs the callback on every render, so you never need to worry about stale closures:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n\n  // This callback always has access to the latest `count` value\n  useHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count)\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nThe hook automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts:\n\n```tsx\nfunction TemporaryPanel() {\n  // Automatically cleaned up when this component unmounts\n  useHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}\n```\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nTo register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic list whose length isn't known at compile time, use the `useHotkeys` (plural) hook instead of calling `useHotkey` multiple times. You can't call hooks conditionally or in loops, so a single hook that takes an array is the way to handle a variable number of shortcuts.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeys } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  useHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nBecause `useHotkeys` accepts a plain array, you can derive it from data:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MenuShortcuts({ items }) {\n  useHotkeys(\n    items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe hook diffs the array between renders by array index plus the normalized hotkey string, registering new hotkeys and unregistering removed ones automatically. Reordering the array changes that identity, so reordered entries are unregistered and re-registered even if their callback references stay the same.\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```tsx\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\nuseHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `useHotkeyRegistrations` hook to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. It's the building block for shortcut palettes and help dialogs.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const { hotkeys, sequences } = useHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        {hotkeys.map((reg) => (\n          <li key={reg.id}>\n            <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n            {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n            {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n      {sequences.length > 0 && (\n        <>\n          <h2>Sequences</h2>\n          <ul>\n            {sequences.map((reg) => (\n              <li key={reg.id}>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            ))}\n          </ul>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe returned `hotkeys` array contains registration objects with the hotkey string, options (including `meta`), and enabled state. The `sequences` array contains sequence registrations with the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nUnder the hood, `useHotkey` uses the singleton `HotkeyManager`. You can access the manager directly if needed:\n\n```tsx\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\n\n// Check if a hotkey is registered\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\n\n// Get total number of registrations\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n\nThe manager attaches event listeners per target element, so only elements with registered hotkeys get listeners. That beats a single global listener that has to inspect every keystroke.\n"}
